The '''History of Sarta (1947–1977)''' covers the postwar settlement that followed the collapse of the wartime regime and the more liberal, reconstruction-oriented era that came after it. It began with Sarta's accession to the [[Adonian Community]] on 11 August 1947, the adoption of a new constitution, and universal suffrage. The new order was built around a broad coalition known as the PUM, formed from the UM, and is generally remembered as one of the more unified and positive periods in modern Sartan history.

The era was marked by postwar recovery, steady population growth, and a sustained attempt to build a new national identity. Sarta aligned its public finance with the international dollar, restored relations with [[Guajicutea]], and tried to fit itself into the wider international economic order. Growth remained slower than in some neighbouring states, and the economy still depended heavily on exports to [[Volisania]] and [[Jacan]], but the political atmosphere was markedly less volatile than in the wartime decades.

== 1947–1955: reconstruction and liberalisation ==

The immediate postwar years were dominated by reconstruction, constitutional reform, and the public exposure of wartime abuses. War tribunals continued into the late 1940s, and the state used the process to distance itself from the old fascist order. Officials also promoted a more inclusive civic identity through schooling, public ceremony, and national memorial culture. The aim was not only to rebuild infrastructure, but also to give the kingdom a postwar identity that could survive the collapse of the wartime regime.

The new constitution widened political participation and formalised universal suffrage. In practice, this produced a much broader electorate and a more open public sphere than Sarta had known under the authoritarian order. The PUM, a broad coalition rather than a narrow faction, became the main vehicle for this reconstruction politics. It tried to hold together royalists, moderates, union organisers, and anti-wartime figures who agreed that the country needed stability more than ideological renewal.

== 1955–1965: export growth and state alignment ==

By the 1950s, Sarta's economy had settled into a pattern of export-led growth. Trade remained heavily oriented toward Volisania and Jacan, and that dependence kept the kingdom tied to outside demand even as domestic institutions became more liberal. Public finance increasingly used the international dollar, reflecting an effort to align Sarta with the wider postwar economic order and make external trade easier to manage.

The state also worked to restore foreign ties that had been broken during the fascist years. Relations with [[Guajicutea]] were reopened, turning a former colonial relationship into a more ordinary diplomatic and commercial one. That restoration mattered symbolically as well as economically: it signalled that the postwar government intended to normalise Sarta's place in the region rather than preserve the isolation and hostility of the previous era.

Growth during these years was real, but it remained constrained by the kingdom's export structure. Sarta was expanding, yet it was doing so more cautiously than some of its neighbours. The result was a relatively stable prosperity rather than a dramatic boom.

== 1965–1972: manufacturing and services ==

In the 1960s, some manufacturing that had previously been based in Volisania began to be offshored to Sarta. Lower labour costs, port access, and a more predictable political environment made the kingdom attractive for industry that was looking for a cheaper base. This shift did not replace the older export economy, but it did begin to reshape it.

At the same time, the decline in coal imports weakened the older extraction trade. Districts and firms that had depended on mining and port handling found that the old model was less profitable than before, and the government increasingly encouraged manufacturing and services instead. Transport, administrative work, and urban services expanded alongside industry, especially in the larger cities.

This diversification was significant because it moved Sarta away from a single export pattern and toward a broader economy. Even so, the country still relied on the wider regional market, and much of its growth depended on demand from the same neighbouring states that had long defined its commercial life.

== 1972–1977: oil shock and recession ==

The era began to unwind in the mid-1970s, when the Camboriú War and the oil shock hit Sarta hard. Manufacturing demand declined, transport became more expensive, and the export economy slowed just as the kingdom was becoming more dependent on industrial and service growth. The downturn was especially severe because the government refused to privatize the affected industries. Instead, it chose to wait out the crisis, keeping state support in place while the recession spread.

That decision softened some immediate dislocation, but it also prolonged the recovery. By 1977, the PUM's long period of control was ending, and the political consensus that had defined the postwar decades had started to break apart. New parties emerged out of the crisis environment: conservative, socialist, progressive, and centrist forces that would shape the next phase of Sartan politics.

Even so, the 1947–1977 period is generally remembered as a constructive one. It rebuilt the state after war, normalised foreign relations, broadened political participation, and gave Sarta a more stable national identity. Its limits were real, especially in the economy, but it left the country far more durable than the exhausted wartime order that preceded it.

